<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Oleśnica</h1><p class="st-meta">1320 CE – 1884 CE (approx.) · capital Oleśnica</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Silesian duchy that outlived its Piast founders by four centuries, passing to the Poděbrady, Württemberg and Brunswick families.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the division of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-glogow">Duchy of Głogów</a> among the sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-of-glogow">Henry III</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-i-of-olesnica">Konrad I</a> received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olesnica">Oleśnica</a> (Oels), east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a>, and ruled it as a separate duchy by 1320. In 1329 he did homage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-bohemia">King John of Bohemia</a>, placing the duchy under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piast</a> dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olesnica">Oleśnica</a> at times also held other Silesian towns, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kozle">Koźle</a> and part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bytom">Bytom</a>. After their line died out in 1492, the duchy went to the Bohemian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-podebrady">Poděbrady</a> family, who introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a>, and in 1647 by marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silvius-i-nimrod-duke-of-wurttemberg-oels">Sylvius Nimrod of Württemberg</a>, whose line made Oleśnica a small centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> court culture and Lutheran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-silesia">Silesia</a> passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1742. In 1792 the duchy went by marriage to the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>, and when the last Brunswick duke died in 1884 it reverted to the Prussian crown, which granted it to the crown prince. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olesnica">Oleśnica</a> became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1945.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-olesnica.jpg" alt="Duchy of Oleśnica" width="320" height="272"><figcaption>Image: Schläsinger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banner_Konrad_des_Wei%C3%9Fen_von_Oels.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1320 CE – 1884 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Oleśnica</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Konrad I, Sylvius Nimrod</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Polish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Poland</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Duchy of Oels, Duchy of Olesnica</dd></dl></aside></div>
